# The rewrites this site needs from its host — the one list. Netlify's _redirects format: a line is
# `source destination status`, `:splat` the rest of a `/*` path, 200 a rewrite (the browser stays on the site's
# domain). $SUPABASE_URL is the project's address (https://<ref>.supabase.co): the build writes dist/_redirects with it
# filled from VITE_SUPABASE_URL, so Netlify takes that file as is; Render's Redirects/Rewrites are these lines typed in
# (action Rewrite, above any catch-all); deploy/nginx/ is generated from this file (npm test refuses drift). The order is
# the order a host that takes the first match keeps: the specific before the general, the app's fallback last.
# `node packages/kit/build/rewrites.mjs --check` (in npm test) refuses a rule a module on this site needs and this file
# lacks, and one for a module that is off.

# EVERY API OF THIS SITE HAS A ROOM OF ITS OWN UNDER /api: one segment named for what the API does,
# never for the product whose shape it wears, and inside it that product's own path continues
# unchanged — so a client changes its base URL and nothing else. No two rooms can collide and no
# rule has to come before another to be correct. A reader's address is never a machine's, which is
# what lets a surface have the obvious name (/spaces is the community, not Contentful).
#
# Three rooms repeat a word — /api/store/api/<version>/…, /api/desk/api/v1/…, /api/forms/forms/… —
# because those products build that part of the path themselves. The repetition IS the
# compatibility; trimming it would rewrite their path. Do not tidy it.

# the content API — Contentful's Delivery, Preview, Sync and GraphQL (api + a content kind)
#   contentful.js: createClient({ host: <site>, basePath: '/api/content' })
/api/content/*       $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/content/:splat                  200

# the media API — the images door, whose urls the content API writes (api + a content kind)
/api/media/*         $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/images/:splat                   200

# the desk — Chatwoot's Client API, Application API and website snippet (api + support)
#   chatwootSDK.run({ baseUrl: '<site>/api/desk' }); the snippet's script is a file of the build
/api/desk/packs/js/sdk.js  /packs/js/sdk.js                                     200
/api/desk/*          $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/support-api/:splat              200

# the scheduling API — cal.com's v2 (api + sessions); the base is <site>/api/scheduling/v2
/api/scheduling/*    $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/scheduling-api/:splat           200

# the forms API — Typeform's (api + forms); @typeform/api-client: apiBaseUrl: '<site>/api/forms'
/api/forms/*         $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/forms-api/:splat                200

# the events API — every event, the site's own and a registered source's, in PostHog's wire
# protocol (always); a PostHog SDK: api_host: '<site>/api/events'
/api/events/*        $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/ingest/:splat                   200
/api/events          $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/ingest                          200

# the store API — Shopify's Storefront endpoint and the handoff's cart read (api + store)
/api/store/*         $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/storefront-api/:splat           200

# THE STORE'S ALIAS, and the reason it is the last rule under /api: @shopify/storefront-api-client
# takes a storeDomain and throws away any path on it — given <site>/api/store it asks for
# <site>/api/<version>/graphql.json regardless. So the same door is served there too, and its
# second segment being a version is why nothing else may sit under /api without a room.
# build/storefront-api.test.mjs pins the client's behaviour, so the alias cannot be removed
# without a failing test saying why it was there.
/api/*               $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/storefront-api/api/:splat       200

# THE SOCIAL NETWORK'S DOORS (the network module). An atproto client — Bluesky's app, or any other —
# asks /xrpc/<method> at the ROOT of the host it was given, and resolves a member's handle at that
# host's /.well-known; neither can carry a room under /api, so these three are the one place a
# protocol's own path stands at the root. A site with the network off has none of them.
/xrpc/*                    $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/atproto/xrpc/:splat                       200
/.well-known/atproto-did   $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/atproto/.well-known/atproto-did           200
/.well-known/did.json      $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/atproto/.well-known/did.json              200

# THE APP CALLING ITS OWN BACKEND IS NOT AN API and has no room: /v1/<function> is the Edge
# Functions gateway on this site's domain (the v1 is Supabase's, not ours), so the browser reaches
# every function same-origin and without a preflight, and a link a function writes into an email
# points at this site rather than at supabase.co. It is what PUBLIC_FUNCTIONS_URL names.
# It can never be renamed: the tracked links, the open pixel and the unsubscribe link in marketing
# email already sent are written as <site>/v1/… and are sitting in people's inboxes.
/v1/*                $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/:splat                          200

# THE SHARE PICTURES, for a host that cannot run a browser. A redirect applies only where no file
# matches, so the build's own photograph wins and only a record published since it reaches the
# function — which draws a card with satori rather than photographing the real one (it cannot: there
# is no browser). Where a prerender sidecar is running it answers instead and the card is the real
# one; nginx implements this rule itself (rewrites.mjs NGINX_OWN).
/share/*             $SUPABASE_URL/functions/v1/share-image?path=:splat           200

# everything else is the app: a prerendered page as it is, else the shell — 200, never a redirect
/*                   /index.html                                                200
